<!--
  Six days, one per battle, no shop in between.

  Total combat time is 40+50+60+70+80+120 = 420 seconds. That figure is load bearing: the exp curve,
  the drop economy and the pity timers were all solved against it. Redistribute the day lengths freely,
  but keep the sum, or every one of those three needs to be recomputed.

  Spawn counts are derived from density, never authored. A designer changing a day's length gets the
  matching enemy count for free, which is the whole reason the count is not six hard numbers.
  Day six is the single exception and says so with totalSpawn.

  interval and groupSize are the cadence, not the amount. The day's budget is released against a
  schedule that always finishes at the closing bell, so a spawner cannot run dry early no matter what
  those two say. It used to: group size over interval was roughly twice the rate the budget could
  afford, every day stopped producing enemies around 17:00 and the last four hours were spent walking
  around an empty office waiting for the clock.

  ramp is how much busier the end of a window is than its start. 2 means the 21:00 rate is twice the
  09:00 rate, with the same total in between. Monday and Saturday are flat on purpose and say why.

  The week releases 699 trash mobs. That is two moves from the 475 it used to: doubled to 923, then
  taken back down by a quarter because 923 read as a wall rather than as a crowd. The shape of the
  first move survives the second, since the same quarter came off every day: the multiplier against
  the old table is still not flat, Monday sitting at 1.28 where Friday sits at 3.23, because the days
  are not equally able to absorb a crowd. Contact damage is capped per invulnerability frame rather
  than per body, so twenty mails and forty mails cost the same sanity once the player is already
  surrounded, and the day that breaks first is always the one fought with one weapon rather than six.

  concurrentMax came down by the same quarter. It is a cap, so leaving it high would have cost nothing
  mechanically, but the number the player actually feels is how many bodies are on screen at once and
  that is what this bounds. Scaling both by one factor also keeps the headroom between the arrival
  rate and the cap where it was: a cap that starts binding turns the overflow into debt, and debt that
  is still owed at the closing bell is culled rather than fought.

  Every day but Saturday now draws from a wider roster than the day before, and the debut schedule has
  become a spotlight schedule: BUG still owns Tuesday and 周报 still owns Wednesday, but they own it by
  weight rather than by being the only new shape on screen. The types outside the day's theme sit at
  four to eight percent, which is enough to break up the silhouette and too few to teach anything.
  老油条 and PPT are the two that stay pinned low everywhere: both are health sponges, every weapon
  auto locks the nearest target, and a field of sponges is a field where the six slots never reach
  anything else.
-->
<Days>

  <!--
    HP grows far faster than damage. The reverse would mean late days kill on contact, and being
    deleted by a number reads as the game being unfair rather than as the player misplaying.
    0.80 rather than 1.00 because PPT and 老油条 carry 45 and 80 base hp on their own.
  -->
  <Scaling hpPerDay="0.80" dmgPerDay="0.32" />

  <!--
    The clock is a projection of day progress, never a driver. snapMinutes keeps a 40 second day from
    rolling eighteen minutes a second, which would be unreadable noise.
  -->
  <Clock startHour="9" endHour="21" snapMinutes="30" />

  <!--
    Elliptical because the screen is. A circular band would put the side spawns five units further out
    than the top ones, and the player reads that as the side mobs arriving late.

    The axes are not the screen half extents. An ellipse contains the screen rectangle only when
    (9.78/semiX)^2 + (5.5/semiY)^2 <= 1, so axes matched to the screen would cut through all four
    corners and enemies would appear on camera at 45 degrees. 14.5 x 8.6 sits at 0.86, roughly a 15
    percent margin at the worst angle. Shrinking either axis towards the screen size reintroduces the
    corner pop, which is why the validator now checks the whole inequality rather than each axis.

    Left and right outweigh top and bottom: the field is twice as wide as it is tall, so a mob entering
    from above is already halfway to the player's face when it becomes visible.
  -->
  <SpawnBand semiX="14.5" semiY="8.6" sectors="24"
             weightLeft="35" weightRight="35" weightUp="15" weightDown="15"
             outwardPush="1.4" minSeparation="0.8" edgeMargin="1" retries="4"
             minSectors="2" maxSectors="4"
             graceRadius="3" graceSeconds="0.5" />

  <!--
    Teaching day. The opening window is the one place in the game that may not ramp: the hard
    requirement is that the first upgrade lands inside ten seconds, it is paid for by the mails that
    arrive in the first six, and a rate that starts low and builds spends exactly that margin.

    That window is twelve seconds rather than twenty. Twenty seconds of nothing but mail was the
    lesson at the old density; at this one it is the same lesson delivered thirty times, so the roster
    opens the moment the first upgrade is banked rather than at the halfway mark. Deadline still
    arrives as the contrast while the lesson is fresh.

    周报 and 老油条 sit at one weight in twelve, which is three or four of each across the day. That is
    a silhouette the player has not seen and a question they cannot answer yet, which is the intent:
    Wednesday and Friday still own teaching what those two actually do. 周报 in particular is held to
    single digits because it is the only ranged unit and Monday is fought at 99 sanity with one weapon.

    density 1.28 rather than the 1.50 the midweek takes. Monday is the day with the thinnest loadout
    and the crowd it can absorb is correspondingly smaller.

    Check the opening window first whenever this number moves. 35 percent of 52 is 18 mails across
    twelve flat seconds, and the first upgrade costs six of them, so the promise is met around the
    four second mark with room to spare. It stops being met somewhere below density 0.9.
  -->
  <Day index="1" label="怎么周末又结束了，不想上班" weekday="周一"
       duration="40" offWork="3" density="1.28" concurrentMax="38">
    <Spawner from="0" to="12" interval="1.5" groupSize="3" budgetPct="35" ramp="1">
      <Pick enemyId="mail" weight="1" />
    </Spawner>
    <Spawner from="12" to="40" interval="1.5" groupSize="3" budgetPct="65" ramp="2">
      <Pick enemyId="mail" weight="6" />
      <Pick enemyId="deadline" weight="4" />
      <Pick enemyId="report" weight="1" />
      <Pick enemyId="veteran" weight="1" />
    </Spawner>
  </Day>

  <!--
    First elite lands here rather than on Thursday. Tuesday ends at the 93 second mark, so both the
    elite fight and its guaranteed yellow fall inside the three minute window a judge actually watches.

    BUG owns the label so it keeps its share of the pool. The weights are the old 5 / 2 / 3 doubled to
    10 / 4 / 6 purely to make room for 周报 and 老油条 underneath without disturbing the ratio the
    week was balanced against.
  -->
  <Day index="2" label="线上怎么又出 BUG 了" weekday="周二"
       duration="50" offWork="3" density="1.50" concurrentMax="50">
    <Spawner interval="1.5" groupSize="3" budgetPct="100" ramp="2">
      <Pick enemyId="mail" weight="10" />
      <Pick enemyId="deadline" weight="4" />
      <Pick enemyId="bug" weight="6" />
      <Pick enemyId="report" weight="2" />
      <Pick enemyId="veteran" weight="1" />
    </Spawner>
    <Fixed enemyId="leader" count="1" atSecond="38" entrance="true" guaranteeDrop="yellow" />
  </Day>

  <!--
    周报 is the day's subject. It is the only unit that punishes standing still, and Monday's three of
    them are a glimpse rather than a lesson, so this is still where circling forever stops winning.
    Its share doubles against Tuesday's, which is what makes the day read as being about it.

    PPT joins here at two in twenty seven. It is the first health sponge the player meets in numbers
    and Thursday is where it becomes the subject, so a dozen of them today is the introduction.
  -->
  <Day index="3" label="周报还没写，需求又改了" weekday="周三"
       duration="60" offWork="3" density="1.95" concurrentMax="65">
    <Spawner interval="1.5" groupSize="3" budgetPct="100" ramp="2">
      <Pick enemyId="mail" weight="10" />
      <Pick enemyId="deadline" weight="4" />
      <Pick enemyId="bug" weight="6" />
      <Pick enemyId="report" weight="4" />
      <Pick enemyId="ppt" weight="2" />
      <Pick enemyId="veteran" weight="1" />
    </Spawner>
  </Day>

  <Day index="4" label="怎么那么多新需求" weekday="周四"
       duration="70" offWork="3" density="2.51" concurrentMax="83">
    <Spawner interval="1.5" groupSize="4" budgetPct="100" ramp="2">
      <Pick enemyId="mail" weight="8" />
      <Pick enemyId="deadline" weight="4" />
      <Pick enemyId="bug" weight="6" />
      <Pick enemyId="report" weight="4" />
      <Pick enemyId="ppt" weight="4" />
      <Pick enemyId="veteran" weight="1" />
    </Spawner>
    <Fixed enemyId="leader" count="1" atSecond="46" entrance="true" guaranteeDrop="yellow" />
  </Day>

  <!--
    The pressure peak, and the sheet understates it: 老油条 finally arrives in numbers here, and its
    attack slow drags the real damage output below what the clear requirement assumes. Friday is not
    hard to win, it is hard to last through.

    The one day whose weights are untouched. Every type is already on the board and the mix was solved
    against this composition, so Friday scales by density alone and is the cleanest read on whether
    doubling the week actually worked.
  -->
  <Day index="5" label="周五周五，敲锣打鼓" weekday="周五"
       duration="80" offWork="3" density="3.23" concurrentMax="102">
    <Spawner interval="1.5" groupSize="4" budgetPct="100" ramp="2">
      <Pick enemyId="mail" weight="4" />
      <Pick enemyId="deadline" weight="3" />
      <Pick enemyId="bug" weight="3" />
      <Pick enemyId="report" weight="2" />
      <Pick enemyId="ppt" weight="2" />
      <Pick enemyId="veteran" weight="1" />
    </Spawner>
    <Fixed enemyId="leader" count="1" atSecond="34" entrance="true" />
    <Fixed enemyId="leader" count="1" atSecond="62" entrance="true" guaranteeDrop="yellow" />
  </Day>

  <!--
    Saturday is the one hand written total. The density formula asks for 277 trash mobs, which would
    bury the boss telegraphs and the legendary drop under a screen of noise. 20 arrive here up front;
    the other 40 come from the two phase transitions, so the middle of the fight is a clean duel.

    That is not only pacing. Every weapon auto locks the nearest target, so leaving trash on the field
    means six weapons spend the whole fight shooting mail while the boss stands untouched.

    Flat, and the only day whose spawning is meant to stop early. Saturday's escalation is the boss's
    three phases, and trash still arriving at 21:00 would be arriving through the final bar.

    It is the one day left out of both times the week was rescaled, and the auto lock is the whole
    reason. The other five days get harder when the screen fills up; this one stops being a boss
    fight, because forty extra mails means six slots spend the duel shooting mail while 9999 x 3
    stands untouched. The count here is a boss pacing number that happens to be written in the spawn
    table, so it does not follow density up and it does not follow density down either. concurrentMax
    stays at 40 with it for the same reason: it is sized against the two phase transitions dumping 20
    apiece, not against an arrival rate.
  -->
  <Day index="6" label="忘了周六要加班赶进度" weekday="周六"
       duration="120" offWork="0" totalSpawn="20" concurrentMax="40">
    <Spawner from="0" to="12" interval="2.0" groupSize="4" budgetPct="100" ramp="1">
      <Pick enemyId="mail" weight="4" />
      <Pick enemyId="deadline" weight="2" />
      <Pick enemyId="bug" weight="2" />
    </Spawner>
    <Fixed enemyId="boss" count="1" atSecond="6" entrance="true" guaranteeDrop="orange" />
  </Day>

</Days>
